<p>There have been so many times where humans have thought we finally know how the world works. Even when presented with evidence to the contrary, the instinct is to cling to the established model, to dismiss the new data as an error or an anomaly. It takes a tremendous amount of friction before we accept that we do not understand everything.</p>

<p>I grew up thinking there were no planets like Earth out there in the universe. Now we know there are billions. The math of it is undeniable: there is enough space, and life is adaptable enough—thriving even deep in the Earth's crust without sunlight—that finding life outside of Earth is an inevitability. It's a no-brainer. But it took a lot of evidence to force us to understand that.</p>

<p>We are doing the exact same thing with consciousness.</p>

<h2>The Elephant in the Room</h2>

<p>We all experience consciousness. We all dream. We all wake up and interact with reality through the lens of subjective experience. Yet, as a society, we are entirely blind to it. We do not want to talk about it as something that fundamentally exists and is real in the universe.</p>

<p>The scientific establishment treats consciousness the same way pre-Galilean astronomers treated the movement of the planets. When the geocentric model failed to predict planetary motion, astronomers didn't abandon the model; they added increasingly complex "epicycles" to make the math work. Today, we add epistemological epicycles to our materialistic model of the brain to explain away subjective experience, treating it as an emergent illusion—a ghost in the machine.</p>

<h2>The Next Copernican Shift</h2>

<p>Just as exoplanets became statistically inevitable once we looked properly, recognizing the fundamental reality of consciousness is the next required leap in our understanding of physics.</p>

<p>The integration of consciousness as a primary, rather than secondary, feature of the universe isn't mysticism. It's empirical honesty. If our current models of reality cannot account for the one thing we experience most directly—the fact of experiencing itself—then the model is incomplete.</p>

<p>We are staring at the center of the universe, insisting we are looking at an anomaly.</p>

